qdev-host (0.6.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * TB bus in macOS guests: hot-unplug as well as hot-plug, repeatedly, with
    stock QEMU. macOS releases a device only through the attention-button
    sequence of a slot with a power controller, and it switches such a slot
    on only when the device's link is down at the hot-plug event. The ports
    of a macOS VM keep their power controller, get x-speed=2_5 and no ACPI
    properties, and each hot-plug starts with a key device (pvpanic-pci with
    x-pcie-lnksta-dllla=off) that makes macOS switch the slot on and then
    leaves on the host side; the real device arrives in the powered slot.
    qdev-host reads the slot state through the PCIe MMCONFIG window.
  * PCI pass-through and vfio-user devices that the hookscript hot-plugs at
    post-start wait in a background job (qdev-host-tb-boot-VMID) until macOS
    has started the ports.
  * qdevctl tb --acpi auto; --acpi thunderbolt or --power-controller off on
    a macOS VM keep the 0.6.1 hot-plug-only mode.
  * Status: a USB device whose TB controller is detached no longer says the
    TB bus appears at the next VM start.
  * Packages the runtime display resize merged after 0.6.1: qdp ABI v3 adds
    an optional ui_info() so a display client's window size reaches plugins
    that implement it (Display1 Console.SetUIInfo is forwarded, control
    command "uiinfo W H [WMM HMM]"); ABI 1 and 2 plugins run as before.

 -- Dmitry R <rdmitry0911@gmail.com>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 23:30:00 +0100

qdev-host (0.6.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * TB bus for macOS guests: macOS waits for the ports' own PCIe hot-plug
    interrupts, but q35 hands bridge hot-plug to ACPI, and a root port's
    power controller keeps a hot-plugged device unpowered until the guest
    switches the slot on, which macOS does not do. For a VM with
    isa-applesmc in its args the bus now adds
    -global ICH9-LPC.acpi-pci-hotplug-with-bridge-support=off and gives its
    ports power_controller_present=off. qdevctl tb --hotplug
    native|acpi|auto and --power-controller on|off|default override it.
    Checked with macOS 26.1: devices on TB ports at the start and hot-plugged
    ones (qemu-xhci, tb-vga, virtio NIC) are configured, USB devices on the
    TB controller plug and unplug; a PCIe unplug completes when the VM stops.
  * A vfio-user device the guest has not released keeps its process until
    the VM stops instead of disappearing under the guest; post-stop stops
    such processes.
  * Removing the last device keeps the VM's TB bus settings while the bus
    stays in its args.

 -- Dmitry R <rdmitry0911@gmail.com>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 17:30:00 +0100

qdev-host (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * TB bus: hot-plug PCIe root ports qdev-tb0..3 (pcie.0 slot 0x09) whose
    ACPI nodes carry the Thunderbolt properties of the qemu-thunderbolt fork
    (PCI-Thunderbolt, pci-supports-link-change) as _DSD and as the _DSM macOS
    reads. The SSDT is built per VM by qdev-host's own AML encoder; ports and
    the devices present at VM start come from a -readconfig file that the
    hookscript writes at every start, so args change only once.
  * QEMU's own devices on the TB bus, added to and removed from the running
    VM over QMP: tb-pci (PCI pass-through, bound and reserved with
    qemu-server's own functions, mappings included), tb-net (virtio, e1000e
    or vmxnet3 on a tap of a node bridge, VLAN tag and rate through
    qemu-server's tap_plug), tb-xhci (qemu-xhci) and tb-usb (a USB device of
    the node on a tb-xhci controller, also for guests such as macOS that do
    not take PVE's own USB hot-plug).
  * qdev-host's own devices go to the TB bus as well: a new tb-vga (or other
    plugin device) is hot-plugged on a free TB port, a new virgl-gpu sits on
    one from the VM start; the bus goes in front of the VM's args so its
    ports exist before the GPU's -device. Devices of earlier versions keep
    their root port qdev-rp0, and --bus qdev-rp0 still adds one.
  * Web UI: PVE's own PCI Device, Network Device and USB Device dialogs get a
    Bus field (Standard or TB bus); TB devices are rows of the Hardware panel
    whose Edit opens the same dialog with their settings. The USB controller
    is a qdev-host Device. qdevctl tb VMID --ports N sets the number of ports.
  * PCI pass-through devices on the TB bus are hot-plugged at post-start:
    QEMU pins the VM's memory for them before it daemonizes, and
    qemu-server's start timeout grows only for its own hostpciN (a 32 GiB VM
    was killed). They go in before vfio-user devices, and are not hot-plugged
    while one is attached: QEMU 11's legacy VFIO container code casts every
    container of the address space and aborts on a vfio-user one.
  * qdevctl tb VMID --acpi thunderbolt|hotplug|none chooses the ports' ACPI
    properties (default thunderbolt); --io-reserve, --mem-reserve and
    --pref64-reserve change the windows the firmware reserves per port.
  * macOS: in a Sequoia-era OpenCore guest no device behind any PCIe root
    port was configured (TB ports in all three modes, PVE's own port too),
    hot-plug and unplug requests were ignored; Linux guests are unaffected.
  * tools/test-tb-bus.py checks the AML encoder and the generated files
    offline; tools/pve-tb-ui-test.mjs drives the dialogs on a node.

 -- Dmitry R <rdmitry0911@gmail.com>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 16:30:00 +0100

qdev-host (0.5.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * virgl-gpu: new setting display=primary|vga|secondary, replacing vga=1|0.
    primary (the default) makes a vhost-user-gpu-pci the VM's only display:
    it has no VGA part, so QEMU 11's crash in vga_update_display() when a
    guest reboots with a console open cannot happen; OVMF's virtio-gpu driver
    draws the firmware screen. vga is the former vhost-user-vga, secondary
    the former vga=0. Devices saved with vga=1|0 keep their mode; the
    Hardware panel offers the three as a list.
  * The guard keeps vhost-user-gpu's 2D resources (OVMF's virtio-gpu driver,
    before the guest's VirGL driver) in shared memory instead of GBM buffers:
    NVIDIA's GBM refused the linear buffer and libgbm crashed the process at
    the first firmware frame. virglrenderer's own GBM device is untouched.
  * virgl-gpu: the vhost-user-gpu guard publishes the GPU's state in
    gpu-state next to its socket (running, stopping, stopped) and, when QEMU
    stops the device, waits up to 1.5 s for the display client named in
    gpu-state.client to confirm in gpu-state.ack that it left the display.
    QEMU 11's side of a vhost-user-vga crashes when a display client
    refreshes the console after a device reset (vga_update_display), and
    loops for good on descriptor -1 when a client's GL update is
    acknowledged while the GPU socket is closed; both need a client attached
    while the GPU is stopped. qsm-pve-direct from 0.4.0+stock4.14~exp236
    attaches only while the GPU runs and leaves within the handshake.
  * A scanout the guest switches off gets a blank surface, as in QEMU's own
    virtio-gpu, so a later reset does not find the console without one.
  * Checked with qsm-pve-direct exp236 on a test VM with a GL D-Bus display
    and the console open: a VM start (the console waits for the GPU, then
    shows it) and a pause and resume (the client leaves within the handshake)
    no longer hit QEMU's faults. A reboot inside the guest still crashes QEMU
    in vga_update_display(): its D-Bus listener outlives the client while a
    GL update is in flight. Not fixable outside QEMU; reported upstream.

 -- Dmitry R <rdmitry0911@gmail.com>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 14:30:00 +0200

qdev-host (0.5.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * virgl-gpu: the GPU process (QEMU's vhost-user-gpu) runs with
    /usr/lib/qdev-host/libqdev-vugpu-guard.so preloaded, which works around
    two faults of it without changing QEMU:
    - a virgl fence signalled while QEMU answers an EDID or display info
      request freed the waiting command, and the reply crashed the process
      (segfault in get_edid_cb); seen with a Plasma guest during a console
      resize. Fence notifications are held until the reply is handled.
    - after a pause (qm suspend, suspend-mode backup, snapshot with RAM) or a
      reboot inside the guest, the process refused QEMU's new GPU socket, the
      display stopped and a later stop ended the process. The new socket is
      put under the old descriptor, and QEMU is answered for while it is away.
    Takes effect when a VM next starts. Not covered: commands running when
    the guest resets the device, and virglrenderer state from the guest's
    previous boot.
  * Includes 0.5.3 (Hardware panel through the TCP 8006 gateway on a page
    opened by IP address).

 -- Dmitry R <rdmitry0911@gmail.com>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 12:10:00 +0200

qdev-host (0.5.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Hardware panel: through the TCP 8006 gateway the panel's requests use the
    page's credentials mode, so the browser sends them on the page's own
    connections. On a page opened by IP address with the certificate's
    warning accepted (the certificate names the node), Chrome rejected the
    certificate on the separate connections of requests without credentials,
    and the panel showed "Failed to fetch". The service still authorises
    only the Authorization header and ignores the PVE cookie.

